93a258077244ea6e6a1a43a9d2e8b8fa41aa6555.

Objective and current boundary
------------------------------

Bring native NetBSD on a single-CPU SGI Octane to a serial multi-user login,
using IRIS for initial development, followed by physical hardware validation.
The first implementation task is an IP30 kernel entry and early-console proof.
Graphics, SMP and IRIX applications follow the native platform milestones.

What has actually run:

* Octane: a standalone big-endian N64 ELF, using ARCS64 to print output,
  inspect argv/environment, enumerate memory and return to the PROM.  This
  retains the PROM stack and exception handlers.  It is NOT a NetBSD kernel.
* O2: the retained NetBSD XIRI_O2 kernel attaches both Adaptec controllers,
  finds sd0, mounts FFS read-only, runs uname/mount in a native single-user
  shell, and halts cleanly.  Multi-user login was not tested in this run.
* Both results use the IRIS interpreter.  Neither physical hardware nor JIT
  operation is qualified by these results.  No Octane NetBSD login exists.

The earlier Octane test tried the O2 kernel.  Its ELF32 wrapper was rejected
with "Can't boot 32 bit ELF"; loading its ELF64 form faulted inside firmware
before NetBSD entry.  GENERIC64_IP3x explicitly targets O2/IP32.  Existing
MACH_SGI_IP30 cases do not implement the missing Octane board support.

