Reproduce the firmware milestone first
-------------------------------------

Run from the NetBSD repository.  Choose new output paths on every run:

    sh xiri/platforms/ip30/build-probe.sh \
      ../xiri/.work/netbsd64-build/tools/bin/mips64--netbsd- \
      /tmp/xiri-ip30-handover-build

    python3 xiri/platforms/probe.py \
      --xiri ../xiri \
      --emulator ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/native-20261115/emulator \
      --machine octane \
      --image /tmp/xiri-ip30-handover-build/arcs64-probe \
      --nvram ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/octane-20261114/inputs/nvram.bin \
      --flash ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/octane-20261114/inputs/flash.bin \
      --expect XIRI_IP30_ARCS64_OK --return-to-prom \
      --output /tmp/xiri-ip30-handover-run

The linker must select -m elf64btsmip: this cross linker's default is N32.
The tested ELF entry/load address is 0xa800000020080000, corresponding to
physical 0x20080000.  It is a diagnostic placement, NOT a decided kernel ABI.
Inspect the ELF headers/program headers; do not substitute the O2 ELF32
wrapper.  The current harness selects one R10000, 512 MB, no graphics and no
disks.  Missing-SCSI firmware diagnostics are expected with this fixture.

Require the success marker followed by the PROM prompt, result status passed,
emulator_stopped=true and originals_unchanged=true.  Read serial.log as well
as the JSON.  Firmware success alone cannot satisfy any kernel milestone.
The harness needs local Unix/control sockets and the emulator's host runtime
libraries.  Use the host/toolbox environment if the sandbox blocks these.
The local toolbox is xiri-iris; retained binaries avoid an initial rebuild.

Architecture decisions required before kernel changes
----------------------------------------------------

1. Firmware ABI.  Current sys/dev/arcbios/{arcbios.h,arcbios.c,
   arcbios_calls.S,genassym.cf} implement 32-bit firmware layouts and an o32
   call bridge, including for N64 kernels.  Add a distinct ARCS64 path selected
   before parsing argv, descriptors or component pointers.  Preserve the
   existing O2/Indy path.  Audit signatures, widths, alignment, return values,
   generated assembly offsets, console, environment, tree walking and reboot.
   Native N64 pointers alone do not make a 32-bit firmware vector compatible.

2. Entry and addresses.  Audit sys/arch/sgimips/sgimips/machdep.c (mach_init
   currently takes argv32), locore_machdep.S, sys/arch/mips/mips/locore*.S,
   sys/arch/mips/conf/Makefile.mips, the sgimips linker/config files, and MIPS
   pmap/physical-page bootstrap.  LP64 currently adds -msym32.  Choose and
   document whether the kernel uses a full-width direct address or a wired
   mapped virtual layout; then audit every affected assembly/symbol/address
   conversion.  Simply changing TEXTADDR on the O2 kernel is insufficient.

3. RAM and vectors.  IRIS models Octane RAM starting at physical 0x20000000.
   The first 16 KiB are accessible at physical zero; CPU access to the
   corresponding high 16 KiB is a bus error, with main RAM accessible from
   0x20004000.  See ../iris/src/dev/heart_mem.rs and verify hardware evidence
   before treating emulator behavior as a physical guarantee.  Reserve the
   firmware/SPB/vector areas, kernel text/data/BSS, bootstrap stack and page
   tables explicitly.  Honor descriptor types, not just total RAM size.
   Audit low exception vectors, cache attributes/flushes, TLB setup, overflow
   and holes, mips_page_physload(), pmap_bootstrap() and kernel-end conversion.

4. Kernel ownership.  The standalone probe borrows PROM state.  A kernel
   must initialize its own stack/BSS and CPU state and safely take ownership
   of exceptions/interrupts.  Establish when firmware calls remain safe and
   transition to an IOC3 console before relying on native interrupt service.
   A deliberate early-entry checkpoint can stop before that takeover, but
   must be reported as an entry proof, not a completed bootstrap.

Observed ARCS64 interface facts for the retained firmware:
  SPB:                 0xffffffffa0001000; signature 0x53435241
  SPB vector offset:   64 bytes; vector entries/pointers are 64 bits
  Version/revision:    16-bit fields; do not widen every field blindly
  Memory descriptor:  32-bit type, base_page at offset 8, 64-bit page count
  Service indices:     GetMemoryDescriptor=18, Write=27, GetEnvironment=30
  Memory total:        0x20000 4-KiB pages (512 MB, including reserved types)
The diagnostic has layout assertions.  Extend these for any additional ABI
structures actually used by the kernel; do not infer them from host layout.

Implementation milestones and acceptance
---------------------------------------

M0 -- Reproduce and record the baseline (first session).
  Run the diagnostic above; retain source/binary hashes, exact command,
  firmware hashes, configuration, full serial output and cleanup result.
  Audit the source areas above and write the chosen address/firmware design.
  If this baseline fails, diagnose the environment or regression before
  layering kernel changes on top of an unknown failure.

M1 -- Dedicated IP30 kernel entry and early console (first code change).
  Introduce a separate minimal serial-only config, proposed name XIRI_IP30,
  under sys/arch/sgimips/conf.  Keep CRIME/MACE/Newport devices out of it.
  Add the necessary entry/address handling and ARCS64 early output path.
  Acceptance: the Octane PROM loads the new NetBSD ELF64, reaches its real
  entry on a kernel-owned stack with initialized BSS, and prints an explicit
  kernel-entry checkpoint plus firmware/memory information.  Capture the
  entry address and CP0/stack state and show that this is the kernel artifact,
  not the standalone diagnostic.  Any intentional early halt is documented.

M2 -- Memory bootstrap, HEART interrupts and timekeeping.
  Implement the remaining memory ownership/VM work, HEART attachment and
  interrupt mask/pending/acknowledge/priority routing.  Select a supported
  clock/timecounter and document the relationship to CPU Count/Compare.
  Acceptance: native exception handling works, RAM reservations are correct,
  timer interrupts advance without a storm, allocation proceeds into normal
  autoconfiguration, and repeated boots give consistent memory accounting.

M3 -- XIO/Crossbow, Bridge and native IOC3 serial.
  Add board bus attachments and PCI configuration/mapping, preserve full
  address width, and implement endian-correct bus_space access.  Implement
  IOC3 serial first (polled early access may be useful before full PCI attach),
  then its interrupt path and the PROM-to-native console handoff.  Keep the
  dependency ordering explicit if minimal polled serial is pulled into M1.
  Acceptance: enumerate the expected PCI functions, identify IOC3 and QLogic,
  and demonstrate native serial input/output and interrupt delivery without
  relying on ARCS calls after takeover.

M4 -- Bridge DMA, QLogic storage and native single-user root.
  Reuse sys/dev/pci/isp_pci.c and sys/dev/ic/isp* for the ISP1040 where suitable;
  implement board bus_dma translation, masks, cache synchronization and
  interrupt routing rather than inventing a second generic SCSI driver.
  Start with a disposable native NetBSD disk and read-only root.  If an
  embedded native ramdisk helps separate CPU/VM/userland from storage, label
  it as an intermediate checkpoint, not disk-root acceptance.
  Acceptance: sd0 discovery, real FFS mount, native /bin/sh, uname/mount,
  repeatable read/checksum checks, and clean halt.  Then validate writes,
  synchronization, cold reboot persistence and filesystem consistency only
  on private copies before enabling writable-root qualification.

M5 -- Native serial multi-user login (this issue's primary boot target).
  Complete RTC/time handling and native tty/getty startup using the actual
  attached serial device name.  Use a native NetBSD userland matched to the
  kernel's supported ABI; the existing O2 fixture uses an N64 kernel with
  o32 userland, so its CHD is a candidate, not proof of Octane compatibility.
  Acceptance: cold boot without manual single-user intervention, login
  prompt, successful native login, uname/mount/date and a small filesystem
  round trip, then clean shutdown and another successful boot.  A screenshot
  of a prompt without a successful login does not complete this milestone.

M6 -- Qualification and later expansion.
  Qualify IOC3 networking, sustained storage/DMA, input and hardware behavior.
  Record the physical machine's CPU, memory, PROM, graphics and PCI inventory
  before hardware testing.  The local IRIX fixture describes a one-CPU
  195-MHz R10000 Octane, 512 MB, Solid IMPACT; that is not a fresh hardware
  inventory.  Track IMPACT/VPro framebuffer, wscons/theme support and native
  Xorg separately.  Newport and O2 CRM are different devices.  SMP and IRIX
  desktops are later work; retain separate 6.5.22/6.5.30 acceptance records.

