Build and test discipline
-------------------------

At handover time there was no working XIRI_IP30 kernel config/build.  M1 has
since added it; see the checkpoint update at the end of this file.  The
planned command below is retained as the handover-era record.  Adapt the
proven O2 build command using NEW output directories, for example:

    octane_build="$PWD/../xiri/.work/netbsd-octane-build"
    ./build.sh -m sgimips -a mips64eb -U -u -j 2 \
      -O "$octane_build/obj" -T "$octane_build/tools" \
      -D "$octane_build/dest" \
      -V MKX11=no -V 'HOST_CC=cc -std=gnu17' \
      -V 'HOST_CFLAGS=-O2 -std=gnu17' \
      kernel.gdb="$PWD/sys/arch/sgimips/conf/XIRI_IP30"

This is a planned command, not a claim that the nonexistent config builds.
Prepare the isolated cross tools first with build.sh's tools target using
the same machine/architecture/output options.  Preserve ELF64 netbsd and
netbsd.gdb, readelf headers/segments, linker map, config and complete logs.
Do not give Octane netbsd.elf32 even if the common build emits one.  Extend
the runner for kernel milestones: --return-to-prom is for the diagnostic,
and its current --single-user option is O2-only.

Regression policy by change:
* Firmware/entry/pmap/cache changes: build relevant Indy and O2 configurations;
  run their native boot/shell baselines as well as the new Octane checkpoint.
* Bus/interrupt/DMA changes: test mapping boundaries, widths/byte lanes and
  interrupt acknowledgement, then exercise the corresponding guest device.
* Emulator changes: keep them in ../iris, with a failing-before device/CPU
  regression and the affected test suite; rerun native guest baselines.
* Shared native promotion: qualify both vendor release lines; current port
  checkpoints alone cannot replace the last qualified desktop artifacts.

The O2 native regression can be run from this repository:

    python3 xiri/platforms/probe.py \
      --xiri ../xiri \
      --emulator ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/native-20261115/emulator \
      --machine o2 \
      --image ../xiri/.work/netbsd-o2-build/tftp/netbsd-XIRI_O2 \
      --nvram ../xiri/.work/donors/irix-6.5.30f-original-media/nvram.bin \
      --disk ../xiri/.work/images/xiri-netbsd-desktop-20261114-controls/netbsd-xiri.chd \
      --expect 'Enter pathname of shell' --single-user \
      --output ../xiri/.work/octane-handover-o2-regression

Run against a stopped source CHD.  The runner creates a private reflink/copy
and overlay; use disk-backed storage, not a large /tmp disk copy.  Check
clean_shutdown, stopped processes and unchanged originals.  Existing logs
retain the original executed probe; do not rewrite them after harness edits.

Known O2 pitfall: db49ed4b fixed IRIS truncating the wide MACE PCI window at
physical 0x280000000 into a low RAM alias.  Controller writes corrupted the
TLB refill vectors, causing a later apparent reserved-instruction trap in
memset.  This was separate from NetBSD's R10000 cache-mask fix 2a7184ec4c6.
For a similar failure inspect vector corruption and full bus addresses before
blaming the instruction at EPC.  Do not assume an Octane failure has the same
cause, or mask it with an unverified kernel workaround.

Every checkpoint should preserve source revisions/dirty state, build command,
config and artifact hashes, emulator/toolchain/features, firmware hashes,
serial and monitor logs, marker meaning, elapsed time, cleanup and unchanged
input checks.  For failures capture PC/EPC, Cause, Status, BadVAddr, registers,
relevant vectors and board state, plus symbolized disassembly around the fault.
Classify it as loader, kernel, emulator or unresolved using evidence.  A
timeout/panic/missing marker is a failed checkpoint, not partial boot success.

