M2a checkpoint update (2026-11-15)
----------------------------------

M2a is the first M2 checkpoint: cache-alias reconciliation, ARCS64 memory
setup and vector bootstrap, stopping before HEART interrupts or a timer.

What now exists:

* sys/arch/sgimips/conf/XIRI_IP30_M2: includes XIRI_IP30, removes
  XIRI_IP30_M1_STOP and continues through ARCS64 and pmap bootstrap.  The
  M1 config remains reproducible with an explicit stop.
* sys/arch/sgimips/include/ip30_arcs64.h and sgimips/ip30_early.c:
  ARCS64 environment, memory and console helpers.  Every firmware call
  saves and restores MIPS_CURLWP (t8); this is not an N64 ABI callee-saved
  register and firmware clobbers it.
* ip30_arcs64_init() sets mach_type=IP30, ncpus=1, the identifiers and an
  ARCS64 console before the generic mach_init paths use them.
* mips_machdep_cache_config() sets both mips3_xkphys_cached and
  mips3_pg_cached to CCA 5 for XIRI_IP30, matching the 0xa800... entry
  alias rather than the generic CCA 3 default.
* Exception vector CPUVAR accesses load &cpu_info_store from a cell at
  physical 0xff0 (the unused tail of the exception block) because vector
  code runs from the low SDRAM alias and cannot reach XKPHYS data with
  compact lui/%lo addresses.  A second cell at 0xff8 holds
  mips3_slowfault, reached with jr instead of a truncated 26-bit j; both
  cells are initialized before vector installation.  The cache-error
  vector uses an uncached XKPHYS alias rather than a truncated KSEG1
  address.  tlb_update_addr now uses PTR_LA, and critical sections that
  wrote Status=0 preserve KX on IP30.
* mips_kernel_vaddr_to_paddr() makes mips_page_physload() handle XKPHYS
  kernel bounds.  ARCS64 memory descriptors feed the existing cluster
  logic; end addresses are computed from the original base plus length and
  rounded inward, empty ranges are skipped, and the
  0x20000000..0x20003fff guard and low firmware alias are not free memory.
* Vector slots are now UTLB 0x7c, XTLB 0x4c, cache 0x3c, general exception
  0x78 (0x7c in XIRI_IP30_M2_SLOWFAULT) and interrupt 0x28 bytes; all fit
  their 0x80 slots.  Vector installation clears BEV without panic.

